<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swedish East India Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1731">1731</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> was a chartered trading company founded in 1731 and based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>. It traded chiefly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, importing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and other Asian goods into northern European markets. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> note that, while commercially marginal in global terms, the company's trade connected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> to the wider Asian commerce of the eighteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swedish-east-india-company.jpg" alt="Swedish East India Company" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZuppaDiCarlo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandiera_della_Compagnia_Svedese_delle_Indie_Orientali.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
